A||N||18||bHooper||Flywheel Removal advice needed...||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||03-18-2000||02:43 PM||||Ok, so call me ambitiouse....<P>I'm waiting...<P>Seriousely, in preperation for the new tranny, I was going to remove the old rusted flywheel and bolts.  Unfortunately, the just spin until the car builds up compresion.  Is there a "trick" to removing the flywheel?  Can one person, with little mechanical skill do this, or do I need to have someone help me???<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||03-18-2000||03:22 PM||||Yes, the old trick is to use a big screwdriver or a tire iron and put the tip into the teeth of the flywheel and pry against something that won't break.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mutfish||03-18-2000||03:23 PM||||There is a flywheel turning/holding tool.Or you can wedge a screwdriver between the block and a tooth on the flywheel to hold it while you turn the bolts..........||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||03-18-2000||03:42 PM||||sounds viable, I'll give it a spin  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||03-18-2000||08:57 PM||||well, i wasnt able to make anything happen.   i think i am going to have to drop the cradle.  i really wanted to try and do everything with the engine still in the car, just drop the drivers side, so i could get the tranny in.<P>i think i am going to spray some more WD-40 on it tonight, let it soak and give it a whirl later this week.  but i dont want to round off the bolts!!<P>of course, it might be helpfull if i new which way to turn the bolts, in relation to the rear of the car!  <P>anyone? anyone? anyone?<p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 03-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||03-19-2000||02:36 AM||||Lefty loosy, righty tighty.  Uh  that would be counter clockwise.  Um  if the wrench handle is pointing at the ground pull it towards the rear of the Fiero to loosen the bolt.||||reg||12
Z||000006||bHooper||03-19-2000||07:33 AM||||thats what i thought, but you never know when the wonderfull world of counterrotation will take effect on a bolt  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||03-19-2000||07:55 PM||||An interesting thought.<P>A friend of mine was working on his car.  His 12 year old son was helping.  My friend told his son to tighten the bolt clockwise.  His son gave a blank stare.  He repeated and got the same "deer in the headlights" stare.  His son finally asked what did he mean by "clockwise".<P>All of their clocks in their house were digital and the son had not a clue what "Clockwise" meant.  What is this world coming to?||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||03-19-2000||08:16 PM||||hehehehehehe!  good one! ||||reg||1
Z||000009||DRH||03-20-2000||11:24 AM||||My 17 year old has trouble with analog clocks. She can tell time on them but gets a little confused when the hour hand is between 2 numbers. Lack of experience...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>On the bolts... I have had good luck using the same chain I lift engines with. Bolt it between one of the pressure plate holes in the flywheel and a tranny mounting hole in the block. Use a 6 point socket to avoid rounding. I use a 1/2 drive 24" breaker bar and sometimes a piece of pipe for leverage. Those bolts often have lock-tite on them and are really tough to break loose.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||03-22-2000||07:16 AM||||I am still haveing difficulty removeing the bolts, as they are also rusted.  <P>Is it ok to use a torch to heat them up?  Or is this to critical of a area to do this?<P>Or instead... heat up the flywheel, as I am replacing it anyway?||||reg||5
Z||000011||DRH||03-22-2000||01:04 PM||||Heating the flywheel probably won't help much. The bolts just pass thru it. The threads are probably siezed in the crankshaft. If someone used the heavy duty lock-tite you may <B>have</B> to use a torch to get them out. Heating the bolts should be OK as long as you don't get too carried away. <B>DO</B> replace them with new ones though!!! You may want to get a tap and cleanup the threads in the flywheel before reassembling also.<P>My biggest concern would be heating the end of the crank (heat transfer thru the bolt) too much and messing up the rear oil seal. Shouldn't be too much of a problem if you do the bolts one at a time and let the whole thing cool down if it starts getting too hot.<P>*** Get a couple more opinions before proceding. Just saying what <B>I</B> would do. I have been known to get carried away with a torch before.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ***<P>An impact wrench can also work wonders if you have access to one.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||bHooper||03-22-2000||01:57 PM||||I will try it, as I plan to change the rear main oil seal anyway.  My father suggested the impact wrench... a tool I know little to nothing about.<P>How long should I leave the torch on each bolt, for it to heat up properly?<P>Thanks<P>hoop<p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 03-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||bHooper||03-22-2000||04:54 PM||||are there any fuel lines that i need to be aware of... in the vicinity of the flywheel????  <P><BR>i do not belive so, but i would hate to blow myself up, so close to getting things working  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||03-22-2000||07:10 PM||||1. get the flywheel holder.<BR>2. position the wrench so you are pulling it thru the bolt circle. (Wrench passes over end of crank.) This shortens the leverage and makes the crank work for you.<P>or use an impact wrench.<P>DO NOT use an impact wrench to tighten the bolts. Use a torque wrench and the flywheel holder. You don't want to damage the new wheel. Tighten the bolts in a star patern same as a tire. <P>USE NEW BOLTS. The bolts should always be replaced. Many of us get lazy or cheap and don't do it but we should.||||reg||1
Z||000015||JIM GOOSE||03-27-2000||10:27 AM||||Get a 12v impact wrench from JCW or whoever, these are only 30-40$ and just plug into your lighter socket. If you havent tried one of these for loosening ornery bolts,nuts or anyhting else, you are in for a treat. And they are good for the lugnuts also . I'm on my second one now & they are great..||||reg||12
Z||000016||bHooper||03-27-2000||10:49 AM||||i'm going to either rent or buy an impact wrench this week to remove the flywheel||||reg||1
Z||000017||bHooper||03-31-2000||08:04 PM||||i can understand why people get cheap... the flywheel bolts are $3 and some change, apiece!<P>doing this tommorow, pulling engine for new tranny... which i was hoping would be ready, but the shop is haveing some "minor" setbacks!||||reg||1
Z||000018||lowCG||03-31-2000||11:13 PM||||I'd use a quality,six point socket as well,a stripped bolt head could be a real bummer.<BR>Good luck!||||reg||1
